      </section>
      <h2 id="nw-configuring-qinq-sriov-proc_configuring-qinq-support" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring QinQ support for SR-IOV enabled workloads<a href="#nw-configuring-qinq-sriov-proc_configuring-qinq-support" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Configure QinQ support for SR-IOV enabled workloads to enable double VLAN tagging on your cluster.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have installed the OpenShift CLI (<code>oc</code>).</li>
      <li>You have access to the cluster as a user with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>You have installed the SR-IOV Network Operator.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a file named <code>sriovnetpolicy-810-sriov-node-network.yaml</code> by using the following content:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: sriovnetwork.openshift.io/v1\nkind: SriovNetworkNodePolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: sriovnetpolicy-810\n  namespace: openshift-sriov-network-operator\nspec:\n  deviceType: netdevice\n  nicSelector:\n    pfNames:\n      - ens5f0#0-9\n  nodeSelector:\n    node-role.kubernetes.io/worker-cnf: \"\"\n  numVfs: 10\n  priority: 99\n  resourceName: resource810"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>SriovNetworkNodePolicy</code> object by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f sriovnetpolicy-810-sriov-node-network.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Open a separate terminal window and monitor the synchronization status of the SR-IOV network node state for the node specified in the <code>openshift-sriov-network-operator</code> namespace by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ watch -n 1 'oc get sriovnetworknodestates -n openshift-sriov-network-operator <node_name> -o jsonpath=\"{.status.syncStatus}\"'"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The synchronization status indicates a change from <code>InProgress</code> to <code>Succeeded</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>SriovNetwork</code> object, and set the outer VLAN called the S-tag, or <code>Service Tag</code>, as it belongs to the infrastructure.
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>You must configure the VLAN on the trunk interface of the switch. In addition, you might need to further configure some switches to support QinQ tagging.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a file named <code>nad-sriovnetwork-1ad-810.yaml</code> by using the following content:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: sriovnetwork.openshift.io/v1\nkind: SriovNetwork\nmetadata:\n  name: sriovnetwork-1ad-810\n  namespace: openshift-sriov-network-operator\nspec:\n  ipam: '{}'\n  vlan: <vlan_id>\n  vlanProto: <vlan_protocol>\n  networkNamespace: default\n  resourceName: resource810"} lang="yaml" />
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;vlan_id&gt;</code> specifies the S-tag VLAN tag, for example <code>171</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;vlan_protocol&gt;</code> specifies the VLAN protocol to assign to the virtual function (VF). Supported values are <code>802.1ad</code> and <code>802.1q</code>. The default value is <code>802.1q</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the object by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f nad-sriovnetwork-1ad-810.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>NetworkAttachmentDefinition</code> object with an inner VLAN. The inner VLAN is often referred to as the C-tag, or <code>Customer Tag</code>, as it belongs to the Network Function:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a file named <code>nad-cvlan100.yaml</code> by using the following content:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: k8s.cni.cncf.io/v1\nkind: NetworkAttachmentDefinition\nmetadata:\n  name: nad-cvlan100\n  namespace: default\nspec:\n  config: '{\n    \"name\": \"vlan-100\",\n    \"cniVersion\": \"0.3.1\",\n    \"type\": \"vlan\",\n    \"linkInContainer\": true,\n    \"master\": \"<vf_interface>\",\n    \"vlanId\": 100,\n    \"ipam\": {\"type\": \"static\"}\n  }'"} lang="yaml" />
      <ul><li><code>&lt;vf_interface&gt;</code> specifies the VF interface inside the pod. The default name is <code>net1</code> because the name is not set in the pod annotation.</li></ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the YAML file by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f nad-cvlan100.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Verify QinQ is active on the node by following this procedure:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a file named <code>test-qinq-pod.yaml</code> by using the following content:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: test-pod\n  annotations:\n    k8s.v1.cni.cncf.io/networks: sriovnetwork-1ad-810, nad-cvlan100\nspec:\n  containers:\n    - name: test-container\n      image: quay.io/ocp-edge-qe/cnf-gotests-client:v4.10\n      imagePullPolicy: Always\n      securityContext:\n        privileged: true"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the test pod by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f test-qinq-pod.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Enter into a debug session on the target node where the pod is present and display information about the network interface <code>ens5f0</code> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc debug node/my-cluster-node -- bash -c \"ip link show ens5f0\""} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"6: ens5f0: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> mtu 1500 qdisc mq state UP mode DEFAULT group default qlen 1000\nlink/ether b4:96:91:a5:22:10 brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff\nvf 0 link/ether a2:81:ba:d0:6f:f3 brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, spoof checking on, link-state auto, trust off\nvf 1 link/ether 8a:bb:0a:36:f2:ed brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, vlan 171, vlan protocol 802.1ad, spoof checking on, link-state auto, trust off\nvf 2 link/ether ca:0e:e1:5b:0c:d2 brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, spoof checking on, link-state auto, trust off\nvf 3 link/ether ee:6c:e2:f5:2c:70 brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, spoof checking on, link-state auto, trust off\nvf 4 link/ether 0a:d6:b7:66:5e:e8 brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, spoof checking on, link-state auto, trust off\nvf 5 link/ether da:d5:e7:14:4f:aa brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, spoof checking on, link-state auto, trust off\nvf 6 link/ether d6:8e:85:75:12:5c brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, spoof checking on, link-state auto, trust off\nvf 7 link/ether d6:eb:ce:9c:ea:78 brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, spoof checking on, link-state auto, trust off\nvf 8 link/ether 5e:c5:cc:05:93:3c brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, spoof checking on, link-state auto, trust on\nvf 9 link/ether a6:5a:7c:1c:2a:16 brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff, spoof checking on, link-state auto, trust off   "} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
